    'intersubjective': '# intersubjective: several people\'s maps in one graph, every entry owned by a subject and seen by a subject.\n# Extends mind-topology: its kinds deep-merge into the base kinds, which gain `of` and `seen_by`.\n[blueprint]\nname = "intersubjective"\nversion = "1"\nsummary = "two or more mind maps in one graph: subjects, bonds, transactions (TA), patterns and attachment; cross-map loops, bridges and gaps show the unseen leverage"\nextends = ["mind-topology"]\nmodules = ["core"]\nprivacy = "local"\nseed = [{ kind = "subject", title = "self", set = { role = "self", consent = "self" } }]\nroutes = [\n  ["who is in the map, their attachment style and its source", "`genome find subject`"],\n  ["what runs between two people (loops, the single link carrying a dynamic, unasked questions)", "`genome graph cycles | bridges | gaps --across of`"],\n  ["which exchanges crossed or carried an ulterior message", "`genome find transaction --where type!=complementary`"],\n]\nplaybook = \'\'\'\n**Perspective comes first.** Every entry (including `bond`, `transaction` and `pattern`) has `seen_by`, the\nsubject whose perception it records, and each mind entry has `of`, the subject whose map it belongs to. Both default to SU-01, the self subject seeded at init. An entry with\n`of ≠ seen_by` is a model of another person as perceived by someone. It is never a claim about their inner\nreality. Write such statements as perception: "I experience Alex as withdrawing when…". Another person\'s own\naccount goes in with `seen_by` set to them, and only if they gave it (`consent=given` on their subject).\n\n**Added kinds.**\n- `subject`: a person. Fields: `role`, a general `attachment` style and `attachment_source`. An `inferred`\n  style is a hypothesis, never a label to use on the person.\n- `bond`: one relationship between exactly two subjects, with `safety` (0–10) and its attachment `dynamic`.\n- `transaction`: one exchange between two people. Its ego-state fields are opti